Amplitude of the particle is SHM,

\mathrm{A}=4 \mathrm{~cm}

At mean position velocity,

\begin{aligned}
& v_{\max } &=10 \mathrm{~cm} / \mathrm{s} \\
\Rightarrow \quad A \omega &=10
\end{aligned}

\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad \omega &=\frac{10}{A} \\
&=\frac{10}{4}=2.5 \mathrm{rad} / \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}

Again when $v=5 \mathrm{~cm} / \mathrm{s}$, then displacement, $x=$ ?
We know that,
$v=\omega \sqrt{A^{2}-x^{2}}\Rightarrow \quad 5=25 \sqrt{4^{2}-x^{2}}\Rightarrow \quad 2=\sqrt{A^{2}-x^{2}}\Rightarrow \quad 4=A^{2}-x^{2}\Rightarrow \quad 4=4^{2}-x^{2}\Rightarrow \quad x^{2}=16-4=12\therefore \quad x=\sqrt{12}=2 \sqrt{3} \mathrm{~cm}$

At initial condition of spring,

M g=k X_{0} \quad \text{...(i)}

When mass $m$ is added, then at equilibrium, $(M+m) g=k\left(X+X_{0}\right)\Rightarrow M g+m g=k X+k X_{0}\Rightarrow M g+m g=k X+M g \quad \text{[from Eq. (i)]}\Rightarrow \quad m g=k X\Rightarrow \quad k=\frac{m g}{X} \quad \text{...(ii)}\therefore$ Time period,

\begin{aligned}
T &=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{\text { Total mass of the block }}{\text { Force constant } k}} \\
&=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{M+m}{m g / X}} \\
&=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{(M+m) X}{m g}}
\end{aligned}

Kinetic energy of the system in SHM,

\mathrm{KE}=\frac{1}{2} m \omega^{2}\left(A^{2}-x^{2}\right)

Potential energy of the system in $\mathrm{SHM}, \mathrm{PE}=\frac{1}{2} m \omega^{2} x^{2}$
According to question, $\mathrm{PE}=\mathrm{KE}$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{2} m \omega^{2} x^{2} &=\frac{1}{2} m \omega^{2}\left(A^{2}-x^{2}\right) \\
\Rightarrow \quad x^{2} &=A^{2}-x^{2} \\
\Rightarrow \quad 2 x^{2}=A^{2} \Rightarrow x &=\frac{A}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{aligned}

Frequency of human heart,

f=\frac{72}{60} \mathrm{~Hz}=\frac{6}{5} \mathrm{~Hz}=1.2 \mathrm{~Hz}

Time period, $T=\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{1.2}=0.83 \mathrm{~s}$
